## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design cross-cloud disaster recovery with strict tenant isolation, governance enforcement, and automated validation—a realistic, high-stakes enterprise scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-cloud provider aliasing and per-tenant state backends
- DR readiness flags and retention policies as code
- Shared module registry usage for multi-tenant DR fabric
- Automated testing with Terratest or kitchen-terraform

## Code Example
```terraform
# Minimal skeleton showing per-tenant aliasing
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "primary"
  region = "us-east-1"
}
provider "google" {
  alias   = "dr"
  project = var.dr_project
}

# Per-tenant state backend
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tenant-${var.tenant_id}-state"
    key    = "primary/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

# DR readiness flag
variable "dr_enabled" {
  type    = bool
  default = false
}

# DR submodule mirrors critical resources to GCP when enabled
module "dr_replication" {
  source  = "../modules/dr-replication"
  count   = var.dr_enabled ? 1 : 0
  providers = {
    aws.primary = aws.primary
    google.dr   = google.dr
  }
}
```

## Testing Strategy
```go
func TestPrimaryCreatesResources(t *testing.T) {
    terraformOptions := &terraform.Options{
        TerraformDir: "../fixtures/primary",
    }
    defer terraform.Destroy(t, terraformOptions)
    terraform.InitAndApply(t, terraformOptions)

    assertResourceCount(t, terraform.OutputRequired(t, terraformOptions, "resource_ids"), 1)
}

func TestDRNoOpWhenDisabled(t *testing.T) {
    terraformOptions := &terraform.Options{
        TerraformDir: "../fixtures/dr",
        Vars:          map[string]interface{}{"dr_enabled": false},
    }
    plan := terraform.Plan(t, terraformOptions)
    assertEmptyPlan(t, plan)
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This checks real-world ability to design multi-cloud patterns with clean module boundaries and governance.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform modules, provider aliases, multi-cloud strategy
- Environment backends and per-environment isolation
- CI gates, version constraints, governance for cloud support

## Code Example
```terraform
provider "aws" {
  alias   = "aws"
  version = "~> 4.0"
  region  = var.aws_region
}
provider "google" {
  alias   = "google"
  version = "~> 4.0"
  project = var.gcp_project
}
provider "azurerm" {
  alias    = "azure"
  version  = "~> 3.0"
  features {}
}
module "monitors" {
  source       = "./modules/monitors"
  cloud_config = {
    aws   = { region = var.aws_region, enabled = true }
    gcp   = { region = var.gcp_region, enabled = true }
    azure = { region = var.azure_region, enabled = true }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-cloud drift in this pattern?
- How would you extend to add a new cloud while keeping tight approvals?

## Why This Is Asked
Designed to test practical skills in modular design, dynamic config-driven provisioning, and CI governance for multi-environment Terraform.

## Key Concepts
- wrapper module tag injection
- dynamic resources via for_each and config.json
- per-env backend keys
- CI gate enforcing prod constraints

## Code Example
```terraform
# wrapper module interface
variable "environment" { type = string }
variable "cost_center" { type = string }
output "injected_tags" {
  value = merge({ Environment = var.environment }, { CostCenter = var.cost_center })
}
```

```json
// config.json
{
  "dev": { "buckets": ["dev-app-data"] },
  "prod": { "buckets": ["prod-app-data"] }
}
```

```hcl
# backend config sketch
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state"
    key    = "env/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate config.json before plan?
- How would you extend to include new environments without touching code?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests how a junior engineer models read-only environments, data sourcing, and per-env state management without touching prod resources. It mirrors real-world patterns at scale at top tech companies.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform data sources for read-only references
- Per-env backends to segregate state
- Avoiding resource creation in isolated environments

## Code Example
```javascript
data "aws_vpc" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "tag:Environment"
    values = ["inventory"]
  }
}
data "aws_subnet" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "vpc-id"
    values = [data.aws_vpc.inventory.id]
  }
}
```

```javascript
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state"
    key    = "inventory/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

```javascript
output "inventory_vpc_id" { value = data.aws_vpc.inventory.id }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify that inventory plans never propose changes to prod data? 
- How would you automate inclusion of inventory in CI without accidentally applying prod changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests multi-cloud, multi-env orchestration, and robust gating. It probes backend scoping, provider aliasing, and policy-as-code integration in a realistic production-like flow.

## Key Concepts
- Per-env Terraform backends and state isolation
- Provider aliasing for multi-cloud deployments
- Policy-as-code gates (Sentinel/OPA) for prod safety
- Wrapper modules for consistent tagging across clouds

## Code Example
```javascript
// backend.tf (Terraform S3 backend per environment)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "org-terraform"
    key    = "state/${var.environment}/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Providers with aliases (multi-cloud)
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
  alias  = "aws"
}
provider "google" {
  project = var.gcp_project
  region  = var.gcp_region
  alias   = "gcp"
}
```

```javascript
// Wrapper module usage for tags
module "shared_tags" {
  source      = "./modules/tags"
  environment = var.environment
  cost_center = var.cost_center
}
```

```javascript
// Simple OPA/rego style policy (example)
package terraform.policy
default allow = false
allow {
  input.environment != "prod"
}
```

```javascript
// CI gate steps (high level)
terraform init
terraform plan -out=tfplan
terraform show -json tfplan > plan.json
# Evaluate plan.json against policy (opa or sentinel)
```
